# Git Commit Author and Committer

When creating git commits in this project, set **both Author and Committer** to **tmwclaxton**. Never leave attribution as `toby`, `tobyclaxton`, or `toby.claxton@*`.

`--author` alone is not enough: Git still records the Committer from local `user.name` / `user.email`, which often shows up as Toby on GitHub.

## How to detect availability

Prefer this identity when any of the following is true:

1. `gh api user --jq .login` returns `tmwclaxton`
2. The remote is under `github.com/tmwclaxton/` (e.g. `origin` fetch/push URL)
3. The user asks to commit/push as tmwclaxton

If none of those apply, fall back to the repository's existing git author / user rules and do not invent an identity.

## Identity to use

```text
tmwclaxton <57643714+tmwclaxton@users.noreply.github.com>
```

## How to apply

Set Author **and** Committer via env vars on every commit (do not rely on `--author` alone):

```bash
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="tmwclaxton" \
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="57643714+tmwclaxton@users.noreply.github.com" \
GIT_COMMITTER_NAME="tmwclaxton" \
GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL="57643714+tmwclaxton@users.noreply.github.com" \
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Commit message here.

EOF
)"
```

- Do **not** run `git config` to change global or local `user.name` / `user.email`.
- Do **not** commit with Author or Committer as `toby`, `tobyclaxton`, or any `toby.claxton@*` email.
- After committing, verify with `git log -1 --format='Author: %an <%ae>%nCommitter: %cn <%ce>'` when attribution matters.
- If a commit was just created in this conversation with wrong Author/Committer and has not been pushed, amend with the same env vars and `--reset-author` (only when amend rules allow).
- If it was already pushed and the user wants attribution fixed, ask before force-pushing; on `main`/`master` only force-push when the user explicitly approves.

## Pushing

Push to the `tmwclaxton` remote/repo when that is the project remote. Do not change remotes unless the user asks.
